\begin_layout Title
Astrophysics - Phase Space Measurements and DM Models
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Weighing the Galactic disk using phase-space spirals II.
 Most stringent constraints on a thin dark disk using Gaia EDR3 - https://arxiv.org/pdf/2105.14030
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Rederiving the paper
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the paper equation (6) is the gravitational potential that governs the system and has in it signal parameters:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\Phi(z|\rho_{h})=\sum_{h=1}^{4}\frac{4\pi G\rho_{h}}{\left(2^{h-1}\times100\text{pc}\right)^{2}}\log\left[\cosh\left(\frac{z}{2^{h-1}\times100\text{pc}}\right)\right].
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This form is not accidental but has a logic behind it.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Starting from the Boltzman equation:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\frac{df}{dt} & =C\left[f\right]\\
\frac{\partial f}{\partial t}+\vec{v}\frac{\partial f}{\partial\vec{x}} & -\frac{\partial\Phi}{\partial\vec{x}}\frac{\partial f}{\partial\vec{v}}=0.
\end{align*}

\end_inset

looking only at the 
\begin_inset Formula $Z$
\end_inset

 direction and assuming steady state:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
v_{z}\frac{\partial f}{\partial z}-\frac{\partial\Phi}{\partial z}\frac{\partial f}{\partial v_{z}}=0.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The connection between the phase space and the matter density could be expressed as 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho=\int dv^{3}f$
\end_inset

,
 and the mean vertical velocity is 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{v_{z}}=\frac{\int v_{z}fd^{3}v}{\rho}$
\end_inset

,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{v_{z}}^{2}=\frac{\int v_{z}^{2}fd^{3}v}{\rho}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
to get the the density also in the same equation we integrate by the velocity:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\int v_{z}d^{3}v\left[v_{z}\frac{\partial f}{\partial z}-\frac{\partial\Phi}{\partial z}\frac{\partial f}{\partial v_{z}}\right]=0.\\
\frac{∂}{∂z}\underbrace{\int v_{z}^{2}fd^{3}v}_{\rho\bar{v_{z}^{2}}}-\underbrace{\int\cancel{v_{z}}d^{3}v\frac{\partial\Phi}{\partial z}\cancel{\frac{∂}{∂v_{z}}}f}_{integration-by-parts}=0\\
\frac{∂}{∂z}\underbrace{\int v_{z}^{2}fd^{3}v}_{\rho\bar{v_{z}^{2}}}+\frac{\partial\Phi}{\partial z}\rho=0\\
\bar{v_{z}^{2}}\frac{∂}{∂z}\rho=-\rho\frac{∂\Phi}{∂z}\\
\frac{1}{\rho}∂\rho=-\frac{1}{\bar{v_{z}^{2}}}∂\Phi
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This can give the following solution for 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\rho=\rho_{0}\exp\left[-\frac{\Phi}{\bar{v_{z}^{2}}}\right]
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Adding to this equation the Poisson equation in the 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset

 direction:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{∂^{2}\Phi}{∂z^{2}}=4\pi G\rho_{0}\exp\left[-\frac{\Phi}{\bar{v_{z}^{2}}}\right]
\]

\end_inset

 we define 
\begin_inset Formula $\psi=\Phi/\bar{v_{z}^{2}}$
\end_inset

,
 and multilply by 
\begin_inset Formula $\psi^{'}$
\end_inset

 so the left hand side is:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
LHS=\psi^{''}\psi^{'}=\frac{1}{2}\frac{∂}{∂z}\left[\left(\psi^{'}\right)^{2}\right]
\]

\end_inset

This yields:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\frac{1}{2}\left[\left(\psi^{'}\right)^{2}\right] & =\int\underbrace{\frac{4\pi G\rho_{0}}{\bar{v}_{z}^{2}}}_{A}\exp\left[-\psi(z)\right]dz\\
 & =-A\exp\left[-\psi\right]+C
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Assuming symmetry in the vertical dimension :
 
\begin_inset Formula $\psi(0)=0,\psi^{'}(0)=0,$
\end_inset

 gives 
\begin_inset Formula $C=A$
\end_inset

,
 and therefore:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\frac{1}{2}\frac{∂\psi}{∂z} & =A\sqrt{1-e^{-\psi}}\\
\frac{∂\psi}{\sqrt{1-e^{-\psi}}} & =2A∂z
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To solve this integral we define the variable 
\begin_inset Formula $s=\sqrt{1-e^{-\psi}}\rightarrow ds=\frac{e^{-\psi}}{2\sqrt{1-e^{-\psi}}}d\psi$
\end_inset

 ,
 and we have 
\begin_inset Formula $e^{-\psi}=1-s^{2}$
\end_inset

,
 so we get:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
2\int\frac{ds}{1-s^{2}} & =2Az+C\\
\text{arctanh}(s) & =Az+C\\
\text{arctanh}\left(\sqrt{1-e^{-\psi}}\right) & =Az+C\\
1-e^{-\psi} & =\text{tanh}^{2}(Az)\\
e^{-\psi}=1-\text{tanh}^{2}(Az) & =\frac{1}{\cosh^{2}(Az)}\\
\psi & \approx\ln\left(\cosh^{2}(Az)\right)\\
 & =2\ln\left(\cosh(Az)\right)
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

